record.content:
Consider an object with ecliptic coordinates: \begin{itemize} \item Longitude: \( l = 30^\circ \), \item Latitude: \( b = 15^\circ \), \item Distance: \( r = 1 \) AU. \end{itemize}
The conversion to rectangular coordinates is given in ecliptic coordinate system: \begin{align*} x &= r \cos b \cos l, \\ y &= r \cos b \sin l, \\ z &= r \sin b. \end{align*}
Convert to radians: \[ l = 30^\circ = \frac{\pi}{6} \approx 0.5236, \quad b = 15^\circ = \frac{\pi}{12} \approx 0.2618. \]
Compute: \[ \cos b \approx 0.9659, \quad \sin b \approx 0.2588, \quad \cos l \approx 0.8660, \quad \sin l = 0.5000. \]
Then: \[ x = 1 \times 0.9659 \times 0.8660 \approx 0.8365, \] \[ y = 1 \times 0.9659 \times 0.5000 \approx 0.4830, \] \[ z = 1 \times 0.2588 \approx 0.2588. \]
Thus, the rectangular coordinates are: \[ (x, y, z) \approx (0.8365, 0.4830, 0.2588) \, \text{AU}. \]
